WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 9.2.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:03.680
When each one wins.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.895
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:05.895 --> 00:00:08.050
able to pick grid bot, LP, both, or

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.445
neither, for a given market and account.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:13.035
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:14.720
First, the key ideas.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.435
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:17.435 --> 00:00:18.960
Then, your checklist.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.122
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:25.211
Neither, same as in the grid module: strong

00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:28.997
breakouts, parabolic moves, major news, thin liquidity.

00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:30.732
Waiting is a position.

00:00:30.732 --> 00:00:34.796
Position size, Small–medium (no gas per action), Larger

00:00:34.796 --> 00:00:38.072
(gas becomes a smaller share of returns).

00:00:38.072 --> 00:00:41.362
Pair, Major pairs listed on your exchange, Tokens

00:00:41.362 --> 00:00:44.952
only traded on-chain; pairs with deep pool volume.

00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:48.961
Fee economics, Low maker fees on your

00:00:48.961 --> 00:00:53.602
exchange, High-volume pools with a good fee

00:00:53.602 --> 00:00:57.067
tier (e.g. 0.05% / 0.3% / 1%).

00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:00.760
Management, Want automation (Grid Bot Builder), Happy

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.522
to set ranges and rebalance by hand.

00:01:03.522 --> 00:01:08.312
Custody preference, Comfortable with exchange custody, Want self-custody.

00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:11.896
Counterparty view, Trust the exchange more than contracts,

00:01:11.896 --> 00:01:14.691
Trust audited contracts more than an exchange.

00:01:15.991 --> 00:01:19.993
Leverage wanted, Futures grid (advanced), Not applicable

00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:22.521
(LP is unlevered unless you borrow).

00:01:23.821 --> 00:01:26.336
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:30.248
ETH/U.S.D.C. concentrated L.P. could earn fees

00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:32.296
on part of the stack.

00:01:32.296 --> 00:01:34.042
Size it as ETH they're willing to sell

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:36.371
higher and U.S.D.C. they're willing to spend lower.

00:01:36.371 --> 00:01:38.686
full due diligence (Module 6) and small size.

00:01:38.686 --> 00:01:41.871
$3k, trades bitcoin/ETH, wants automation to grid bot.

00:01:41.871 --> 00:01:47.186
Gas would eat a small on-chain L.P.

00:01:47.186 --> 00:01:52.346
$80k, holds ETH long-term, self-custody,

00:01:52.346 --> 00:01:56.421
checks weekly to a wide-range.

00:01:56.421 --> 00:01:58.569
A token that's only liquid on a

00:01:58.569 --> 00:02:01.151
decks to only an L.P. is possible.

00:02:01.151 --> 00:02:01.826
Apply.

00:02:03.126 --> 00:02:04.586
Here's your checklist.

00:02:04.586 --> 00:02:06.121
Do it now, for real.

00:02:06.121 --> 00:02:08.700
First, I've matched the tool to position

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.516
size, pair, custody and management time.

00:02:11.516 --> 00:02:14.241
Next, I've compared expected fee income

00:02:14.241 --> 00:02:16.721
to costs (gas or exchange fees).

00:02:16.721 --> 00:02:19.946
And finally, I've checked the regime before choosing either.

00:02:21.246 --> 00:02:22.456
Question 1.

00:02:22.456 --> 00:02:25.031
Why does position size matter more on-chain?

00:02:29.451 --> 00:02:31.572
The answer: Gas is a roughly fixed

00:02:31.572 --> 00:02:33.632
cost per action, so it's a bigger

00:02:33.632 --> 00:02:35.511
percentage of a small position.

00:02:36.811 --> 00:02:38.056
Question 2.

00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:40.321
When is an LP the only option?

00:02:44.741 --> 00:02:47.416
The answer: When the pair trades only on-chain.

00:02:48.716 --> 00:02:49.986
Question 3.

00:02:49.986 --> 00:02:52.371
Market just broke out on huge news.

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:53.626
Grid or LP?

00:02:58.046 --> 00:03:00.762
The answer: Neither, until a new range forms.

00:03:02.062 --> 00:03:04.577
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:04.577 --> 00:03:08.147
Next up, Lesson 9.3: Building a combined system.
